  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1011 PM CST TUE NOV 24 2026\n\nIsentropic lift is underway across Central Alabama as evident by\na couple patches of light rain moving across the CWA. If we take\na look at water vapor imagery, we can see our next upper trough\ndigging across east Texas. This trough will bring widespread rain\nWednesday into Wednesday night, during Thanksgiving Eve travel,\nas lift and mid to upper level moisture increases.  At the\nsurface however, winds will remain from the E/NE, with relatively\ndry conditions. With that said, recent forecast soundings do hint\nat a steady moistening of the lower levels through the day\nWednesday which suggests we could see a few periods of heavy\nrainfall. At this time, we are not expecting any strong to severe\nstorms with this system. Most locations can expect to see between\n0.5-1.5\" of rainfall through Wednesday night. Rain looks to\nfinally come to an end late Wednesday, leaving most of\nThanksgiving dry. However, we will carry some low chances across\nour northern tier of counties to account for wrap-around\nmoisture.\n\nA cold front clearing the area Wednesday night into Thursday\nmorning will leave us with a dry and chilly Thanksgiving. We will\nhang onto a low chance for showers across our northern areas to\naccount for wrap-around moisture but most locations are likely to\nremain dry. A breezy northwesterly wind will help keep high temps\nin the mid 40s to low 50s on Thursday. This will also allow\novernight lows to drop into the mid 20s to low 30s Friday and\nSaturday morning. Southerly flow quickly resumes on Saturday,\nbumping temps up several degrees. Ensembles aren't clear for\nSunday: some show another trough/front quickly moving in with\nwidespread rain while others are either slower with the arrival\nor less rainy if at all. The official forecast currently reflects\nlow to medium chances for rain on Sunday.\n\n95/Castillo",
